  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nSome pesky showers lingered across Door and Kewaunee counties \nearly this morning. A few of the meso models that picked up on \nthis activity after midnight has the rain ending between 3 to 5 \nAM. Activity should be confined to the lakeshore counties, \npossibly as far west as Brown and Calumet counties. \n\nMeanwhile, the other concern early this morning is the potential\ndense fog. Enough wind and low stratus has so far prevented \ndense fog to develop. With longer nights and winds may slacken\noff towards sunrise, potential exists for areas of dense fog\nto expand. Any fog should burn off by mid-morning. \n\nFor the Labor Day Weekend, high pressure and upper-level ridging \nwill build over the area today and then remain across the area \nthrough Labor Day as the stretch of above normal temperatures \ncontinues. Current thinking is that the MET guidance is too cool \nwhile the MAV guidance may be a tad bit high. For highs through \nLabor Day, trended toward the BCConsall guidance values. The next \nchance of showers and thunderstorms will be from later Monday \nnight through Tuesday night as a weak cold front moves across the \narea. It is too early to determine if any of the storms would \nbecome severe. Mainly dry conditions are expected Wednesday \nthrough Friday.",
